# network.env — per-site network settings for provision-card.sh.
#
# THIS FILE IS AN EXAMPLE. Copy it outside the repository:
#
#     cp scripts/network.env.example ~/.rpi0-secrets/network.env
#     $EDITOR ~/.rpi0-secrets/network.env
#
# provision-card.sh reads $SECRETS_DIR/network.env (default
# ~/.rpi0-secrets/network.env) and, if a static address is configured
# there, writes it onto the card as a networkd drop-in at
# /etc/systemd/network/20-wlan0.network.d/10-static.conf.
#
# Everything here is OPTIONAL. With no network.env at all, the card keeps
# the image's own default, which is DHCP, and the board is reachable on any
# LAN it can associate with. That fallback is deliberate: the safe path is
# what you get when something goes wrong.
#
# Nothing in this file is a secret — an IP address is public information
# the moment the board sends a packet. It lives outside the repo for the
# other reason: it describes the SITE, not the product, and an image with a
# site's address compiled into it only works at that site.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY BOTHER: THE MEASUREMENT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On the Zero W, going static removed about 4 seconds from time-to-
# reachable, and it did NOT move the number systemd-analyze prints. Both
# halves of that sentence matter, and notes/22 is about why.
#
# The 4 s was never the router being slow. Over five instrumented boots the
# FIRST DHCP exchange failed every single time — 5/5, so not a race, a race
# sometimes wins. networkd then waits out RFC 2131's retransmission backoff,
# measured at 4 +/- 1 s of uniform jitter, and the retry succeeds in about
# 25 ms. So the cost was one lost packet plus a standards-mandated pause.
#
# A static address does not make that conversation faster. It deletes the
# conversation. Claiming an address is a local netlink call to the kernel,
# not a request to anybody: there is no packet that can be lost and no timer
# to roll dice against. Which is why the saving is deterministic rather than
# the quantised 4-or-9 seconds DHCP was giving us.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEFORE YOU USE ONE: RESERVE IT ON THE ROUTER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A LAN has no authority that grants addresses. The host simply claims one
# and answers ARP for it; DHCP is a bookkeeping convention that hosts opt
# into, not an enforcement mechanism. So a static address the DHCP server
# does not know about is an address it will happily hand to somebody else
# once the board's old lease ages out — and then two hosts answer the same
# ARP query, with no referee. The symptom is intermittent and asymmetric and
# looks exactly like flaky hardware.
#
# Reserve the address for the board's MAC on the router. On ASUS that is
# LAN -> DHCP Server -> "Manually Assigned IP around the DHCP list". The
# reservation does not speed anything up — the board is not asking any more
# — it just stops the server giving the address away.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SETTINGS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The address, WITH a prefix length. The prefix is not decoration: it is how
# the kernel decides which addresses are on the local wire (reachable
# directly by ARP) and which have to go via the gateway.
STATIC_ADDR=192.168.50.34/24

# Where anything outside that prefix is sent. Must be inside the address's
# subnet — provision-card.sh refuses otherwise, because a gateway outside it
# produces a next-hop ARP can never resolve, and therefore a board that
# boots perfectly and is silently unreachable.
#
# Use the literal word "none" for a deliberately LAN-only board. There is no
# way to simply omit this: forgetting a gateway and choosing not to have one
# produce the same file but very different intentions, and the script will
# not guess which you meant.
STATIC_GW=192.168.50.1

# Resolver. Defaults to STATIC_GW if unset, since on a home LAN the router
# is usually also the DNS server — that is what DHCP was handing us here.
# Set it explicitly for anything else (1.1.1.1, 9.9.9.9, a local resolver).
#STATIC_DNS=192.168.50.1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE HYBRID, IF THIS BOARD EVER MOVES BETWEEN NETWORKS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# provision-card.sh writes DHCP=no, i.e. pure static, because that is the
# honest configuration for a device living on one known LAN. If the board
# ever needs to survive being plugged into an unknown network, networkd will
# do both at once — hand-edit the drop-in on the card to:
#
#     [Network]
#     Address=192.168.50.34/24
#     DHCP=ipv4
#
#     [Route]
#     Gateway=192.168.50.1
#     Metric=2048
#
# The static address is configured immediately at carrier, so time-to-
# reachable stays where it is; DHCP runs alongside in the background and it
# no longer matters whether it takes 4 s or fails outright.
#
# The Metric=2048 is the non-obvious part, and it is backwards from what you
# would naturally write. networkd gives DHCP-learned default routes metric
# 1024, and LOWER wins. Leaving the static route at its default metric would
# make it preferred everywhere — including on a network where 192.168.50.1
# does not exist, where it would be installed, preferred, and then blackhole
# every packet leaving the LAN. Pushing it to 2048 makes DHCP win wherever a
# DHCP server exists and leaves the static gateway as the fallback for when
# none does.
